<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of the Congo</h1><p class="st-meta">1960 CE – present · capital Brazzaville</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Central African republic west of the Congo River, independent from France in 1960, Marxist-Leninist from 1969 to 1992, led by Sassou Nguesso since 1997.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/middle-congo">Middle Congo</a> became the Republic of the Congo in 1958 as an autonomous colony within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-community">French Community</a>, and became fully independent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 15 August 1960. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulbert-youlou">Fulbert Youlou</a>, an anti-communist who had been the first black mayor elected in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a>, became the first president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Youlou was ousted by a three-day uprising, and the military installed a civilian government under Alphonse Massamba-Débat. Marien Ngouabi took the presidency on 31 December 1968 and proclaimed the People's Republic of the Congo a year later, with the Congolese Labour Party. After Ngouabi was assassinated in 1977, Denis Sassou Nguesso became president in 1979 and aligned the country with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-bloc">Eastern Bloc</a>. Pascal Lissouba was elected in 1992, and a civil war in 1997 ended with Angolan intervention and Sassou declaring himself president.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Sassou won the 2002 election with almost 90% of the vote and the 2009 election, which the Congolese Observatory of Human Rights said was marked by fraud and irregularities. A referendum in October 2015 changed the constitution to let him run again in 2016, which the opposition, who boycotted it, called fake. Fighting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninja">Ninja</a> rebels in the Pool region in 2016 ended in a ceasefire in December 2017.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-the-congo.jpg" alt="Republic of the Congo" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_the_Congo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1960 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Brazzaville</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fulbert Youlou, Alphonse Massamba-Débat, Marien Ngouabi, Denis Sassou Nguesso, Pascal Lissouba</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic, Awakening Churches and Christian Revivalism, other Protestant, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Kituba, Lingala</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Congo-Brazzaville, People's Republic of the Congo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=republic-of-the-congo" aria-label="Republic of the Congo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Republic of the Congo lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g03000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3758 5365L3815 5086"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3815 5086h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3758 5365h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3758 5365h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Brazzaville. <a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=republic-of-the-congo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
